<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-seed-of-an-injected-design-already-stands`>>

!! The seed of an injected design already stands
* `CustodyHomes = { identity, storage, seal }` and `vesselCustodyHomes()` (vault-carriers.ts:123) is the ONE constructor that reads the resolvers. `FsCustodyIo`, plain-rows, main.ts:341 and seven test files already receive or take that record.
* `sealHome` already travels as a PARAMETER through about 25 calls inside open-node-vessel.ts (:1136 reads `larSealHome()` once, then threads it: carriedSet, readKeptAdmitBundles, kahuProtectedHolder, composeDialPresentation…).
* `loadLaresConfig(path = laresConfigPath())` and `daemonBagsDir(cfg = loadLaresConfig())` take injectable config. `placeReadingWatch` already freezes the login once.
* `scratch-custody-setup.ts` refuses unless all three custody homes resolve under the temp dir: the guard for the injected record exists as a test rule.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/what-an-injected-homes-design-replaces`>>

!! What an injected-homes design replaces (map)
# A `Homes` record (extend `CustodyHomes`): `{ identity, storage, seal, state, cache, config, run, data(lares), lararium, corpusRoot, bags, genesis, cas, operator: { login, displayName } }`, built ONCE at the opener from env/XDG/homedir/git (the only place those are read), then passed down. `vesselCustodyHomes()` becomes the record's custody slice.
# `identityDir()` (node-vessel-identity.ts) takes the record; the 14 `readLocalOperatorHint` reads become one read of `homes.operator`, taken at open and recorded beside the key (cures F-1).
# `withLarRoot` (152 sites) and the `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL`/`HOME` env stubs retire: a test builds `Homes` over `mkdtemp` and passes it. The "NOT thread-safe" hazard goes with it, which also unblocks `isolate: false` and parallel in-process vessels (C5).
# The cli `--root` env mutation (found, read, rotate-place, sense HOME swap) becomes an argument to the same constructor.
# `readBackStandsIn` becomes a record field (safety note above); `LAR_CAS` spelled twice becomes `homes.cas`.
# Mesh needs nothing (already env-free). mempalace `xdg-base` is the third source of the same LAR_ROOT/XDG logic that vessel-paths duplicates (:80-123 vs xdg-base:30-72); fold into one pure `resolveHomes({ env, home, tmp })` function that both call, with the three env-reading call sites at the edge. This is a capability-named port (homes) with no host name.
Lift target: substrate adapter (the single env/git/homedir reader) plus pure (`resolveHomes`, the join-only derivations). The `operator` hint is a substrate read behind a named port ("names the local operator"); a browser vessel would supply `{login:null}` or a passkey-derived label.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/house-laws-touched`>>

!! House laws touched
Capability not host (F-1 is the live violation); no key on a surface (unaffected: the key file name moves, the key does not); no global now (the 2 s `git` timeout decides a login); old shapes burn (the login-suffixed file naming is an old shape: lean to retire it, see fork); web3-only (git config is local truth, not a web2 bridge, fine); one owner per registry (xdg-base and vessel-paths both own the LAR_ROOT/XDG rule).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/risk`>>

!! Risk
* Hot-file family: open-node-vessel.ts (ralias holds a 72-line hunk, road owner law :312), node-vessel-identity.ts (held by the founding walk), vault-carriers.ts. Needs T2 (fixture) first per the road; fold into T12.
* Order dependence: `identityDir()` is called before the opener runs in cli verbs (`lares vessel found`); the constructor must run at each verb's edge.
* Failure-mode flip: today an unset/odd git config silently selects another key file; the injected design must REFUSE (named) when the recorded login differs from the one read, instead of silently adopting either. That is a format cut: existing vessels keyed `.vessel-key-<login>.json` need the login read once and recorded (or the suffix retired in the same commit; alpha line: no back-compat arm).
* Hidden: about ten tools/*.mjs witnesses mutate LAR_ROOT; they must follow or keep a thin env shim at the CLI edge.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/red-first-shape`>>

!! Red-first shape
Red: open a vessel with injected homes under a tmp record while the process env points at a poisoned `HOME`/`LAR_ROOT`/`GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL` (a file naming another login); assert no byte lands under the poisoned homes and the key file name comes from `homes.operator`. Today the same test reds because the resolvers read env. Control: the env-driven path with identical homes stays green until retired; a second control: two vessels in ONE process, concurrent (not sequenced), keep separate identity dirs (today: stomp). Assert the setup took by reading the record inside the test. F-1 vector: `login` changes between two calls in one open; expect a named refusal.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/verdict-t16-needs-ruling`>>

!! Verdict T16: NEEDS-RULING.
Forks: (1) retire the login suffix on custody file names (lean: yes, one key per identity dir; the dir already is per-operator and per-LAR_ROOT; the suffix serves "shared machines" only) vs record it at open (lean second if the operator keeps shared-machine use); (2) whether `readBackStandsIn` stays env/marker-driven in tests or becomes a record field (lean: field); (3) sequence: fold into the stage-cut (C1) or land first as the T2/T16 pair (lean: land T16 after T2 and before the stage-cut, since the stage-cut's Ports record needs `Homes` as its first member).

