<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-bridge-catalogforviews-and-bridgebooks`>>

! The bridge — `catalogForViews` and `bridgeBooks`

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/span`>>

!! Span (open-node-vessel.ts, re-found)
&#58;862 comment "(the bridge until O1, `face-fleet-view`)"; :863 `let catalogForViews: (() => LarDoc | undefined) | null = null;`; :864-865 `faceFleetViews(() => (wornFaceProven ? faceBootTiddlers : null), () => custodyGroup, () => (catalogForViews ? bridgeBooks(catalogForViews(), kahuCanon) : new Set<string>()))`; :866 teardown. The ONE assignment: :2035 inside `openDaemon`: `catalogForViews = () => assembly.catalogHandle.doc() as LarDoc | undefined;`. Only consumer: the closure at :865. I read face-fleet-view.ts whole (≈150 lines) and the call sites.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/what-it-does`>>

!! What it does
`faceFleetViews(...)` seats one scoped `SessionDocumentView` per same-operator peer session. For the vessel's WORN face (peer's proven PersonaGroup equals `custodyGroup`) the gate answers `view.fetch(doc).ok || books().has(doc)`. `books()` = `bridgeBooks(catalog, kahuCanon)`: the allow-list of every document ID named by an `automerge:` pointer in a non-face-scoped catalog row, plus the Kahu canon docs (<code>&#64;lares</code>, <code>&#64;lararium</code>). Any document not on that list is shut by default. So the mounted face reads the vessel's wikis, drafts, catalog, oracle and realm pointers across the wire; a foreign face reads its own planes only.
State: `catalogForViews` is a closure-captured `let`, late-bound by `openDaemon` (order dependence: before `openDaemon` runs, `books()` is the empty set, which fails CLOSED; a second `openDaemon` call rebinds to the new assembly's catalog). `bridgeBooks` re-walks the whole catalog on every `mayFederate` call (O(catalog) per document offer; no cache). `wornFaceProven`, `custodyGroup`, `kahuCanon` are also captured lets.
Tests: `face-fleet-view.test.ts` (:132-177, bridgeBooks directly with CONTROLs: a face-scoped row stays out, a realm pointer comes in, canon with/without alias), `kahu-canon-guard.test.ts:64-70` (the alias/after-removal case). The late-bound `catalogForViews` wiring itself has NO test except through a whole node boot.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/does-a-ruling-retire-it`>>

!! Does a ruling retire it?
No ruling retires it; the road OWES its replacement and names no ruling that changes the plan.
* Road F1 (:579): "the face-fleet-view bridge stands as a narrow share caller today, and O1 replaces it with per-surface facets that keep an owner from offering a session island a document beyond them … a wiki island's verbs that read the catalog document move to the view's catalog ask. O1."
* Road O1 (:760-768), the compose-hunk row: "The scoped document view's compose hunks: the per-surface facets in place of the face-fleet-view bridge (today's narrow share caller), the leaf's one-way gate and the hearth's twin." Status: owed. O1's header says "the browser holder and its surfaces wait on W's cutover", and F6 slice four stays owed.
* face-fleet-view.ts header: "A BRIDGE UNTIL O1 … O1 replaces the bridge with per-surface facets." Commit f7ea58433 introduced it as deliberate.
* test-recut-road.mem has no row naming it (grep for bridge/O1/face-fleet: none).
So the bridge sits on the road as a named, deliberate stopgap whose cure is O1's per-surface-facet compose hunk (owner-law territory), NOT a cut candidate for the test re-cut.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/collision`>>

!! Collision
ralias (~/.lares-scratch/ralias/wt) edits face-fleet-view.ts (adds `faceOf`, stores the proven face per view), open-node-vessel.ts (+72 lines, wiring `peerFace: faceViews.faceOf(peerId)` and `sessionFace: faceViews.gateFor(peerId)` into a new vessel-private guard) and kahu-realm.ts; it keeps `catalogForViews` untouched. A cut now collides head-on with ralias and with the owner law (road :312). u1 does not touch these files.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/lift-reading`>>

!! Lift reading (capability not host)
The right stage is a mesh-side cap-stack stage "serves a gate": `SessionDocumentView` is already in mesh; the bridge's two node-only parts are the catalog read (`assembly.catalogHandle.doc()`, a handle) and the Kahu canon ids. Both are ports ("lists the vessel's books", "names the canon docs"). A browser vessel taking a node-like role later would supply the same two. The `let catalogForViews` late bind is a symptom of composing before the assembly exists; a `books: () => ReadonlySet<string>` port supplied by the daemon open would remove the shared `let` without touching the allow-list law.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/security-reading`>>

!! Security reading (no fault found)
Default shut (null books = empty set; a closed view refuses; the worn-face test uses the caller's custody group, then the bootstrap pin). The allow-list admits every non-face-scoped catalog pointer to the worn face's same-operator sessions, by design ("only the mounted face reads the vessel's wikis, catalog and bags"). The comment concedes a vessel "tags no wiki, catalog or bag document by face today". The standing risk is the one the road already holds: a second face on the same vessel sees none of the books; a document that LATER becomes face-private but keeps a non-face-scoped catalog title would ride the bridge (ralias's vessel-private guard is exactly that fix and is in flight). Not filed as a finding; named so the lane brief watches it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/red-first-shape-2`>>

!! Red-first shape (if O1 ever cuts it)
Red: a surface mounted under face A offers a document that only face B's wiki holds; today the worn-face bridge lets it through when B's pointer sits in the catalog under a non-scoped title. Control: face A's own plane document passes. Pin `faceFleetViews` with a `books` port stub, not the whole boot.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/verdict-do-not-cut-the-bridge-belongs-to-o1-s-co`>>

!! Verdict: DO-NOT-CUT (now). The bridge belongs to O1's compose hunk under the owner law; ralias is editing the same hunk; no ruling retires it. Row for the road, not the re-cut: add a characterization test of the late-bound wiring (`books()` empty before `openDaemon`, rebinding on a second `openDaemon`) when B1 pins the boot order.

